1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
All right, let's talk about

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:03,180
something that's all around us, but

3
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:05,060
we rarely stop to really think

4
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:07,560
about. Interest rates. They're kind

5
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:08,720
of like the hidden language of

6
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,180
money. They're in your savings

7
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:11,600
account, your student loans, your

8
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,000
credit cards, and you know what?

9
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:14,620
Understanding them is one of the

10
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:16,240
biggest keys to making smarter

11
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:18,420
financial moves. So let's dive in.

12
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:20,260
So let's kick things off with a

13
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:21,800
little puzzle. You see these

14
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,460
letters all the time, right? APR on

15
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:26,760
a credit card, EAR on a savings

16
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,740
account. They look pretty similar.

17
00:00:28,740 --> 00:00:30,980
Just a percentage. But the

18
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:32,100
difference between them can

19
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:33,780
literally save or cost you

20
00:00:33,780 --> 00:00:35,900
thousands of dollars. So what's

21
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,400
really going on here? Okay, first

22
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,020
things first. APR and EAR are

23
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:43,320
absolutely not interchangeable. You

24
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:45,340
can think of one as, say, the

25
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:46,740
sticker price. And the other?

26
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,460
That's what you actually end up

27
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:50,320
paying. The huge difference between

28
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:51,840
the two comes down to one

29
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,400
incredibly powerful idea.

30
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,660
Compounding. So here's the

31
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:57,360
breakdown. APR, or annual

32
00:00:57,360 --> 00:00:59,160
percentage rate, is just the simple

33
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,120
base interest rate for a year. It's

34
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:03,220
that sticker price I mentioned. But

35
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:05,880
it completely ignores the effect of

36
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:08,440
compounding. EAR, the effective

37
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,700
annual rate, is the real rate. It's

38
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:12,480
what you actually earn or pay after

39
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,380
all that compounding magic happens.

40
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:17,760
So what even is compounding? It's

41
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:19,660
beautiful in its simplicity. It's

42
00:01:19,660 --> 00:01:21,480
just your interest earning its own

43
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,400
interest. The absolute best way to

44
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:24,700
think about it is like a snowball

45
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:26,620
rolling down a hill. It starts

46
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:28,460
small, but as it rolls, it picks up

47
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:30,620
more snow, gets bigger, and starts

48
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:32,780
rolling faster and faster. That's

49
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:33,880
exactly what your money does when

50
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,660
it compounds. It grows on itself,

51
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:37,580
and that growth just accelerates.

52
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:39,580
So how do you get from that sticker

53
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:41,780
price, the APR, to the real deal,

54
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,060
the EAR? It's actually a pretty

55
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:46,200
simple two-step dance. First, you

56
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,020
just divide the APR by how often

57
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:50,120
your money compounds, say by 12 if

58
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:51,920
it's monthly. That gives you the

59
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:53,780
rate for each little period. Then,

60
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:55,600
you let that periodic rate do its

61
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:56,940
thing for the whole year. You

62
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:58,640
compound it. And out comes your

63
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:01,360
true rate, the EAR. And check this

64
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:03,160
out in a real example. Let's say

65
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:05,320
you have a 6% APR that compounds

66
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,260
every month. Well, you're not just

67
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:08,920
getting 6% back at the end of the

68
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:10,520
year. Because your interest is

69
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:11,800
earning interest every single

70
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,840
month, your effective annual rate,

71
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:17,660
your EAR, is actually 6.17%. That

72
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:19,200
little difference? That's the

73
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,040
snowball effect in action. And it's

74
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:23,000
exactly why the EAR is always going

75
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,600
to be higher than the APR if your

76
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:25,860
money compounds more than once a

77
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,360
year. OK, theory is great, but

78
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:29,580
let's put this knowledge to the

79
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:31,420
test in the real world. I'm talking

80
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:32,700
about one of the most common things

81
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:34,520
people borrow money for, a car

82
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:37,460
loan. So picture this. You find the

83
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:40,500
perfect car, and it's $30,000. You

84
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:41,780
take out a loan for the full amount

85
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:44,060
over 60 months. That's five years.

86
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:45,660
The bank hands you the cash today,

87
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:47,320
and your job is just to make 60

88
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:48,780
equal payments to pay it all back.

89
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:51,200
Simple, right? So what does that

90
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:52,620
actually look like month to month?

91
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:56,720
Well, at a 6.75% APR, with that

92
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,720
interest compounding monthly, your

93
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:00,780
payment works out to be exactly

94
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:03,820
$590.50. That's the number that's

95
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:05,340
going to leave your bank account on

96
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:07,320
schedule every single month for the

97
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:09,800
next five years. But here's the

98
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:10,780
part that'll make you sit up

99
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,080
straight. Over those five years, on

100
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:14,560
top of the $30,000 you borrowed,

101
00:03:15,060 --> 00:03:18,660
you end up paying an extra $5,430

102
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:21,420
in interest alone. That's the power

103
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:22,900
of compounding when it's working

104
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:24,940
against you. It turns that innocent

105
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:26,480
-looking percentage into a very,

106
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:28,560
very real cost. Kind of makes it

107
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:29,780
feel a lot more tangible, doesn't

108
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:32,120
it? OK, so we've got to handle on

109
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,400
how these rates work. But where in

110
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,100
the world do they even come from?

111
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:38,540
Who decides what the number is? To

112
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:40,340
answer that, we've got to zoom way

113
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:42,540
out, from our personal finances to

114
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:45,220
the entire economy. And that brings

115
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,060
us to another super important

116
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:49,160
distinction, the nominal rate.

117
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:50,280
That's the number you see

118
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,960
advertised everywhere. But you know

119
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,660
what else is happening? Inflation.

120
00:03:53,900 --> 00:03:55,460
It's always in the background, kind

121
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:56,880
of eating away at the value of your

122
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,280
money. The real rate is what's left

123
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:00,540
over after inflation does its

124
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:02,640
thing. So it's not just about how

125
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:04,380
fast your money grows. It's about

126
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:05,800
how fast your purchasing power

127
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:08,360
grows. And lucky for us, there's a

128
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,480
really easy rule of thumb here. To

129
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:12,140
figure out your real return, you

130
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:13,720
just take that nominal rate and

131
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:14,900
subtract whatever the rate of

132
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,020
inflation is. So if your savings

133
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:19,360
account is paying you 3%, but

134
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:21,880
inflation is at 2%, well, your

135
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:23,680
actual purchasing power only went

136
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:26,140
up by about 1%. That's the number

137
00:04:26,140 --> 00:04:28,640
that really, truly matters. But it

138
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:30,060
gets even cooler than that.

139
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:31,760
Interest rates don't just tell you

140
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:33,640
about what's happening today. They

141
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:36,080
can actually hold clues about where

142
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:37,780
the entire economy might be headed

143
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:39,900
in the future. So how do we read

144
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:41,800
these tea leaves? We read them

145
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:43,020
using a tool called the yield

146
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:44,600
curve. Seriously, you can think of

147
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,100
this thing as the economy's crystal

148
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:48,080
ball. All it does is plot out

149
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:48,940
interest rates for different

150
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,560
lengths of time, from short term to

151
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:52,360
long term. And the shape that line

152
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:53,920
takes, well, that's where things

153
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:56,320
get really interesting. You see,

154
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:58,460
the shape of that curve tells a

155
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:00,200
story. If it's steep, that usually

156
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:01,720
means people are optimistic and

157
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:03,860
they expect economic growth. But if

158
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:05,720
the curve gets inverted and that

159
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:07,460
means short term rates are actually

160
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:09,760
higher than long term ones, that is

161
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:11,540
a classic flashing red light

162
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:12,980
warning sign for a potential

163
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:15,920
recession. OK, let's zoom back in

164
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:17,680
and bring this back home to you.

165
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:19,420
We've talked about the big picture,

166
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:20,780
but interest rates are also

167
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:23,460
incredibly personal. The exact rate

168
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:25,460
that you get offered depends on a

169
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:27,000
couple of final, really important

170
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,880
factors. First up is risk, and this

171
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:31,620
makes perfect sense. Lenders see

172
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:33,460
the US government as basically risk

173
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:35,280
free, so it gets to borrow money at

174
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:37,200
the lowest rate. A super stable

175
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:38,660
company like Johnson & Johnson,

176
00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:40,560
they pay a little bit more. And a

177
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:42,060
company with more uncertainty like

178
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:43,980
Gap, they have to pay a much higher

179
00:05:43,980 --> 00:05:46,340
rate. It's a fundamental rule. The

180
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:47,800
higher the risk, the higher the

181
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:49,060
interest rate lenders are going to

182
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:51,780
demand. And finally, we got to talk

183
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:53,380
about the one thing nobody loves,

184
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:55,860
taxes. They take a big bite out of

185
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:56,960
the interest you earn on your

186
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:58,540
savings and investments. But

187
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:00,000
there's a flip side. If the

188
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,260
interest you're paying on a loan is

189
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:02,620
tax deductible, like a mortgage

190
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:04,560
often is, it actually lowers the

191
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:06,060
real cost of borrowing that money.

192
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:08,560
Just look at this. Let's say you

193
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:10,000
make a great investment and get an

194
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,940
8% return. Awesome, right? But if

195
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:15,400
you're in a 40% tax bracket, you

196
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:17,760
don't actually keep all of that 8%.

197
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:19,960
Nope. 40% of your earnings go

198
00:06:19,960 --> 00:06:21,880
straight to taxes, which means your

199
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:25,400
true after-tax return is only 4.8%.

200
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:27,500
That's a huge difference. And it's

201
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:28,720
a perfect reminder that the

202
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:30,120
headline number isn't what you

203
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:32,860
always get to take home. Wow. OK.

204
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:35,000
We've covered a lot. Compounding,

205
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:38,320
inflation, risk, taxes. But how do

206
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:39,740
we pull all of this together into

207
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:41,700
something useful? Well, there is

208
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:43,900
one final, powerful concept that

209
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:46,020
can basically guide every single

210
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:48,260
financial decision you make. And

211
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:50,180
it's called the opportunity cost of

212
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:51,980
capital. I know, it sounds fancy,

213
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:53,900
but the idea is simple. It's just

214
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:55,200
the best return you could get

215
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,600
somewhere else on an investment

216
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:58,160
that has a similar amount of risk.

217
00:06:58,680 --> 00:06:59,820
This becomes your personal

218
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:01,480
benchmark, the ruler you measure

219
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:03,000
every new financial opportunity

220
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,680
against. And this isn't some Wall

221
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,500
Street theory. It applies to

222
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:09,900
everything. Paying off a 5% car

223
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:11,640
loan early, you're not just getting

224
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:13,460
out of debt. You are, in effect,

225
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:15,560
giving yourself a guaranteed, risk

226
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:17,820
-free 5% return on that money. And

227
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:19,320
if you can't find a better, safer

228
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:21,080
return anywhere else, then that's a

229
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:23,180
fantastic financial move. This

230
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:24,720
concept is the ultimate yardstick

231
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:26,820
for your money. And that brings us

232
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:29,060
to our final and most important

233
00:07:29,060 --> 00:07:31,060
question. The next time you have to

234
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:32,500
make a choice with your money, big

235
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:34,600
or small, just stop and ask

236
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,200
yourself this. What is the absolute

237
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:39,980
best available return for my next

238
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:41,580
dollar? If you can answer that

239
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:43,160
question, you're on the path to

240
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:44,920
building a much stronger financial

241
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:46,680
future. Thanks for tuning in.

